  "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=0,$ then the rational term would be $\\frac{7}{0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $x$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =\\frac{7}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot 14 & =x\\cdot \\frac{7}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot 14 & =\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{x}} \\\\ 14x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14x & =7 \\\\ \\frac{14x}{14} & =\\frac{7}{14} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution.  Thus, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
